        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="16.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#the-short-answer" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. The Short Answer</a>
          <a href="#batteries" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Battery Platforms</a>
          <a href="#build-quality" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Build Quality and Feel</a>
          <a href="#by-category" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Category by Category</a>
          <a href="#price" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Price and Availability</a>
          <a href="#verdict" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. The Verdict</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          You are not choosing a drill. You are choosing a battery you will be buying for the next decade, so the question worth answering is which ecosystem fits the work you actually do.
        </p>

        <h2 id="the-short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          1. The Short Answer
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Choose DeWalt</strong> if you want the widest tool range in North America, the strongest service network, aggressive kit pricing, and a battery system with genuine backwards compatibility through FLEXVOLT. It is the pragmatic default and the one that is easiest to buy into at any budget.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Choose Makita</strong> if you use tools all day and care about weight, balance, vibration and noise. Its LXT platform is the deepest single-battery lineup on the market, its brushless motors are exceptionally efficient, and the difference in how a Makita feels after six hours is not a subtle one.
        </p>

        <p>
          What neither answer captures is that both are excellent, and that anyone who has committed to one and built up a battery stock has very little reason to switch. The comparison matters most for someone starting from zero.
        </p>

        <h2 id="batteries" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          2. Battery Platforms
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">DeWalt 20V MAX and FLEXVOLT</h3>

        <p>
          DeWalt runs one core platform, 20V MAX, and it has run it since 2013 with full backwards compatibility throughout. A pack bought a decade ago still works in a tool bought this year, which is a genuinely valuable property and one that not every manufacturer has managed.
        </p>

        <p>
          Layered on top is FLEXVOLT: packs that reconfigure their cells automatically to deliver 60V in a FLEXVOLT tool and 20V in a standard one. This is the cleverest thing either company has done with battery architecture, because it lets a table saw or a large circular saw exist on the same platform as a compact drill without splitting the ecosystem. There is also POWERSTACK, using pouch cells rather than cylindrical ones for more output from a smaller pack.
        </p>

        <p>
          The practical upshot is that a DeWalt buyer never has to think about compatibility. Every pack fits every tool, and the only decision is capacity.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Makita 18V LXT and 40V XGT</h3>

        <p>
          LXT is the older and larger of the two, and with well over two hundred tools it is the deepest single-battery lineup anywhere. If a cordless version of a tool exists at all, there is a reasonable chance Makita makes it on LXT. Makita also offers 36V tools that run on two LXT packs in series, which extends the platform's ceiling without a new battery.
        </p>

        <p>
          XGT is a genuinely separate 40V system introduced for high-demand applications — large outdoor equipment, heavy rotary hammers, industrial tools. It performs, and it is where Makita is putting its development effort. But it shares nothing with LXT, and that is the single most important thing for a buyer to understand. There is no adapter, no upgrade path, no cross-compatibility.
        </p>

        <p>
          So the Makita decision is really two decisions: which platform, and then which tools. DeWalt's is one decision. That is a real advantage for anyone who does not want to think about it.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Battery Life and Longevity</h3>

        <p>
          On charge cycles and long-term capacity retention, Makita has the better reputation and, in our experience, deservedly so. LXT packs that have been in service for years tend to hold usable capacity better than comparable packs from most competitors, and the charger's active cooling is part of why — it manages pack temperature during charging rather than simply pushing current.
        </p>

        <p>
          DeWalt's packs are perfectly good and its rapid chargers are fast. But over a five-year horizon with heavy cycling, the Makita packs are the ones we would bet on still being useful.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Chargers</h3>

        <p>
          A small point with real daily consequences. Makita's rapid chargers actively blow air through the pack while charging, which keeps cell temperature down and is a significant part of why its batteries age well. A standard LXT pack comes back to full in around half an hour on the rapid charger, and it does so without arriving hot.
        </p>

        <p>
          DeWalt's fast chargers are quicker on paper and get a depleted pack usable sooner, but they rely on passive cooling and packs come off the charger noticeably warmer. In heavy cycling that heat is what shortens pack life. If you charge two or three times a day, this is worth more attention than the charge-time figure printed on the box.
        </p>

        <h2 id="build-quality" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          3. Build Quality and Feel
        </h2>

        <p>
          This is where the two brands diverge most clearly, and where personal preference legitimately decides the answer.
        </p>

        <p>
          Makita builds tools that feel engineered rather than assembled. They are lighter than equivalents — often noticeably so — better balanced, quieter, and produce less vibration through the handle. Pick up a Makita impact driver and a DeWalt one and the difference is immediate. Over a full working day it compounds into genuinely less fatigue, and that is the argument its users make most often.
        </p>

        <p>
          DeWalt builds tools that feel robust. They are heavier, they run louder, and they are generally more tolerant of being dropped off a scaffold or left in a wet truck bed. The housings are thicker, the chucks are stouter, and the general impression is of something designed around abuse rather than around comfort.
        </p>

        <p>
          Neither philosophy is wrong, and neither is a compromise on the other's terms. A finish carpenter holding a tool at shoulder height all day wants the Makita. A demolition crew that treats tools as consumables and replaces them on a schedule wants the DeWalt. Most people are somewhere in between and will be genuinely happy with either, which is why this comparison so rarely produces a decisive winner.
        </p>

        <h2 id="by-category" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          4. Category by Category
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Drills and Impact Drivers</h3>
        <p>
          Close, with a slight DeWalt edge on peak torque and a clear Makita edge on weight and control. Makita's impact drivers have industry-leading assist modes that prevent screw cam-out and self-tapping fastener over-drive, which matters more in practice than the torque figure on the box.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Circular and Track Saws</h3>
        <p>
          DeWalt takes this on raw capability, largely because FLEXVOLT lets a 60V pack drive a full-size saw through hardwood without hesitating. Makita's track saws are superb for finish work and cabinetry, but for sustained framing cuts, DeWalt has more to give.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Outdoor Power Equipment</h3>
        <p>
          Makita, decisively, and specifically on XGT. Its cordless outdoor range is deeper and better developed than DeWalt's, and the 40V platform was built for exactly this load profile. If cordless mowers, blowers and chainsaws are part of the plan, this alone may settle the question.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Rotary Hammers and Concrete</h3>
        <p>
          DeWalt on FLEXVOLT, comfortably. The 60V rotary hammers do work that would otherwise need a cord, and dust extraction integration is well developed across the range.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Finish Carpentry and Nailers</h3>
        <p>
          Makita. Lighter tools, better balance, and cordless nailers that fire consistently without the ramp-up delay that afflicts some competitors. For trim work specifically, this is the platform most finish carpenters we know have settled on.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Range Breadth</h3>
        <p>
          Makita on LXT tool count, DeWalt on how many of those tools you can buy locally without ordering. Those are different kinds of breadth and the second one is often more useful.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Grinders and Multi-Tools</h3>
        <p>
          Makita's angle grinders have the better electronic brake and its anti-restart protection is more thoroughly implemented, both of which matter for safety rather than performance. DeWalt's multi-tools have the wider accessory ecosystem thanks to the universal fitting, though both now accept most third-party blades.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Lighting and Site Equipment</h3>
        <p>
          A category people forget until they need it. Both platforms cover work lights, radios and fans, and both are perfectly competent. Makita's site radios have a following among people who care about such things; DeWalt's tower lights are more common on larger commercial sites. Neither should influence a platform decision, but it is worth knowing that both ecosystems extend well beyond tools that spin.
        </p>

        <h2 id="price" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          5. Price and Availability
        </h2>

        <p>
          DeWalt is generally cheaper to enter, and it discounts far more aggressively. Its holiday kit pricing is genuinely good, and a two-tool combo with two batteries and a charger frequently lands at a price Makita does not match. Bare tools are also easy to find on promotion.
        </p>

        <p>
          Makita holds its price better, both new and used. It rarely runs deep discounts, and the practical consequence is that entering the platform costs more but a Makita tool sold on second-hand retains noticeably more of its value than a comparable DeWalt.
        </p>

        <p>
          Availability is where the gap is widest in North America. DeWalt is in every big-box store, every hardware store and every online retailer, with parts and service to match. Makita's retail presence is thinner and skewed towards specialty suppliers, which is fine if you have one nearby and frustrating if you do not.
        </p>

        <p>
          Outside North America the picture reverses in many markets, where Makita is the default professional brand and DeWalt is the challenger. It is worth checking your own market rather than assuming.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">The Real Cost of Entry</h3>

        <p>
          It is worth doing this arithmetic before committing, because the headline tool price is a small part of it. A realistic starting kit is a drill, an impact driver, a circular saw, a reciprocating saw, three or four batteries in mixed capacities, and a fast charger. On either platform that is a meaningful sum, and roughly a third of it is batteries.
        </p>

        <p>
          Batteries are also the part you keep replacing. A pack in daily professional use has a working life of perhaps three to five years, so over a decade you will buy the battery stock two or three times over. That recurring cost is the strongest argument for taking Makita's longevity advantage seriously, and equally the strongest argument for DeWalt's aggressive pack pricing. Run the numbers for your own usage rather than accepting either brand's framing.
        </p>

        <p>
          The other cost nobody accounts for is time. Waiting a week for a part, or driving across town to the one dealer that stocks a specialty tool, is a real expense for anyone working to a schedule. This is where DeWalt's distribution advantage in North America turns into money rather than convenience, and it is the single most common reason contractors we have spoken to give for choosing it over the tool they actually prefer holding.
        </p>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24 my-16 bg-gradient-to-br from-gray-950 to-gray-900 rounded-3xl p-8 lg:p-12 text-white shadow-2xl">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-white mt-0 mb-6 border-none">6. The Verdict</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-200 mb-4">
            For a professional buying a first platform in North America, <strong class="text-white">DeWalt</strong> is the lower-risk choice. One battery system, everything backwards compatible, FLEXVOLT covering the heavy end, parts on the shelf everywhere, and pricing that lets you build the kit out quickly.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-200 mb-4">
            For anyone whose hands are on tools all day, or whose work is finish carpentry, cabinetry or outdoor equipment, <strong class="text-white">Makita</strong> is the one that keeps paying you back. Lighter, quieter, better balanced, with batteries that outlast the competition and the deepest single-platform lineup available.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-200 m-0">
            If you genuinely cannot decide, go and hold both. This is one of the few comparisons where five minutes in a store settles it better than any amount of specification reading.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Comparing two drills instead of two platforms</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">The drill is the cheapest decision you will make in either ecosystem. What matters is the twenty tools and six batteries you buy over the following decade.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Assuming the higher voltage number wins</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">DeWalt's 20V MAX and Makita's 18V LXT are the same nominal 18 volt platform measured differently. Peak versus nominal voltage is a marketing convention, not a performance gap.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Ignoring what your local store stocks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">The best platform is worthless if the specialty tool you need is a two-week order. Walk both aisles at the retailers you actually use before committing.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Buying into XGT without understanding it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Makita's XGT is a separate 40V platform, not an upgrade path from LXT. The batteries do not cross over, and buying one tool from each means owning two chargers and two battery stocks.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Chasing kit discounts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A five-tool kit is only a bargain if you need five tools. Two good bare tools plus batteries you will actually use beats three that live in the case forever.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Is DeWalt or Makita better?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Neither is clearly better; they optimise differently. DeWalt tends to win on raw power, breadth of range and North American availability, while Makita wins on ergonomics, weight, refinement and battery longevity. For most buyers the deciding factor is which one their local retailer stocks properly.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Are DeWalt and Makita batteries interchangeable?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">No. The battery interfaces are proprietary and physically incompatible, and third-party adapters void warranties and bypass the communication between pack and tool. Choosing a platform is a genuinely long-term commitment.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Makita 18V the same as DeWalt 20V MAX?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Essentially yes. Both are five-cell lithium packs with a nominal 18 volts. DeWalt quotes the peak voltage measured with no load, Makita quotes the nominal working voltage. It is a labelling difference rather than a power difference.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What is Makita XGT and should I buy it?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">XGT is Makita's separate 40V platform aimed at high-demand tools and outdoor equipment. It is genuinely more capable than LXT at the top end, but it shares no batteries with LXT. Buy into it only if the tools you need are XGT-only.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Which brand has better warranty service?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Both offer three years on most cordless tools. DeWalt has the wider service network in North America and generally faster turnaround; Makita's network is thinner in some regions but its repairs are well regarded. Check what exists near you rather than trusting the headline.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is better for a homeowner?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">DeWalt, marginally, on availability and kit pricing. Both are more tool than most homeowners need, and someone doing occasional weekend work would be equally well served by a value platform at a third of the cost.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Which brand is lighter?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Makita, consistently and by a noticeable margin on comparable tools. It is the single most reported difference among people who have used both, and it matters more the longer the working day.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Does DeWalt FLEXVOLT work with regular 20V tools?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Yes. FLEXVOLT packs switch automatically between 60V and 20V depending on the tool, so they run standard 20V MAX tools as high-capacity packs. That backwards compatibility is one of DeWalt's genuine structural advantages.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

